{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Except for his near of kin.</b> 'His near of kin' — this is his wife, for he has closeness of flesh with her. 'His near' — and not a betrothed woman. 'To him' — and not a divorced woman; so did the Rabbis of blessed memory expound. And even though the priest is called holy and Scripture warns him, because of the greatness of his rank, not to defile himself for the dead, Scripture nonetheless commands him that he shall defile himself for his wife, and if he did not wish to, they compel him against his will — as the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: there was an incident involving Yosef the priest whose wife died on the eve of Passover and he did not wish to defile himself, and they defiled him against his will. And this defilement means that he stands in the house under one tent; and the same law applies to his mother and to his father and all the rest; but he may not defile himself and enter the cemetery for any one of them at all — and so the Rabbis of blessed memory said: who has ever seen a priest in the cemetery?" "<b>For his mother and for his father.</b> He placed the mother before the father, whereas in the case of the High Priest he placed the father first, saying (Leviticus 21:11): ״לאביו ולאמו לא יטמא״ — 'for his father and for his mother he shall not defile himself.' And it is possible to say that the reason he placed the mother before the father here is that he had mentioned 'his near of kin' and permitted defilement for it; therefore he needed to place the mother first, in order to juxtapose 'his mother' to 'his near of kin' and to mention the females on their own — for had he said 'except for his near of kin, for his father and for his mother,' the expression would be disordered, inserting the male between the females. Or he placed the mother first because she is definitely his mother, in order to derive a fortiori: no need to state 'his mother' — but even for his father, about whom it is possible to have doubt whether he is his father, he shall defile himself. But in ״לא יטמא״ he placed his father first, for no need to state 'his father' who is a doubt — but even for his mother who is certain, he shall not defile himself. 'And for his sister, a virgin' — excluding one who was raped or seduced; 'who is near to him' — to include his betrothed sister; 'to him' — to include a mature woman; 'who has not been married to a man' — to include a מוכת עץ (a woman who has suffered an injury causing physical perforation). And the law has already been decided that one defiles oneself for these."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:35.887902484Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}